ProsperoSunshine: write the state file atomically, and get past a broken one

A tester's console hung, and afterwards sunshine_state.json was 12464 bytes of zeros. Upstream writes the file in
place without a sync, so a hang or a power cut during or shortly after a write can leave such garbage. From then on
load_state() gave up without a uniqueid (/serverinfo advertised an empty <uniqueid/>), and save_state() refused to
write over a file it could not read, so no new pairing was ever kept.

Writes now go to <file>.tmp, are synced and renamed over the file, and the directory is synced after. A file that
can't be parsed is moved to <file>.corrupt (only the newest is kept) and the host goes on as without a file: a new
uniqueid and no pairings, written at once. save_state() and save_user_creds() write what is in memory instead of
refusing.

--- a/src/file_handler.h
+++ b/src/file_handler.h
@@ -51,4 +51,19 @@
    * @examples_end
    */
   int write_file(const char *path, const std::string_view &contents);
+
+  /**
+   * @brief Write a file so that a crash or a power cut leaves either the old or the new contents, never a mix.
+   * @details The contents go to `<path>.tmp`, are synced to disk and then renamed over the file.
+   * @param path The path of the file.
+   * @param contents The contents to write.
+   * @return ``0`` on success, ``-1`` on failure.
+   */
+  int write_file_atomically(const char *path, const std::string_view &contents);
+
+  /**
+   * @brief Move a file that can't be read to `<path>.corrupt`, replacing an older one, so it is kept for a look.
+   * @param path The path of the file.
+   */
+  void set_aside_corrupt(const std::string &path);
 }  // namespace file_handler
